---
import DocumentLayout from '../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';
import LeadStrand from '../components/LeadStrand.astro';
import Needs from '../components/Needs.astro';
import Draft from '../components/Draft.astro';
---

<DocumentLayout
  title="Choosing the model was the design decision"
  metaTitle="Word web — model evaluation study | Tyler Young"
  description="Twelve models evaluated on quality, cost, latency and format conformance, and why the production choice went to quality."
  descriptionDraft
  eyebrow="02 · Shipped Product"
  meta={[
    ['Role', 'Design and product · two-person team'],
    ['Product', 'Live · [NEEDS: URL + decision to expose]'],
    ['Evaluated', 'Twelve models · July 2026'],
    ['Axes', 'Quality · cost · latency · format conformance'],
  ]}
>
  <p class="answer">
    <Draft>
      Word pages that compose themselves in front of the reader. The interface streams,
      so the model is not a backend detail — it sets the tempo of the page, the texture
      of the writing, and the cost of every request. I evaluated twelve and made the
      call.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <div class="shot">
    <Needs block ratio="16 / 9">
      The evaluation matrix — table or image, sanitized to Tyler's comfort level.
      Model names in or out is his call.
    </Needs>
  </div>

  <h2>The decision</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Read by hand, not scored by another model. The incumbent won on coverage and
      overall quality. Several alternatives matched it closely enough on paper and
      lost on responsiveness — a page that composes in front of you cannot pause to
      think. The final call was a three-way trade between price, responsiveness and
      creative quality, and it resolved toward quality.
    </Draft>
  </p>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      That is the uncomfortable version of the answer. The cheaper option was
      genuinely close, and choosing it would have been defensible on a spreadsheet.
      It was wrong on the one axis the product is actually made of.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>Why this is in a design portfolio</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Because it is a design decision, and because most design decisions never had a
      per-request price attached. Picking a model sets what the interface can promise
      and what it costs to keep promising it. Treating that as someone else's call is
      how products end up fast and thin, or good and unaffordable.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>What it is</h2>
  <p>
    <Needs>
      Tyler confirms the public framing in his own words — product and business, not
      children's media
    </Needs>
  </p>
  <p>
    <Needs>
      Tyler's take: 2–3 sentences on designing for content that streams and composes
      live, where the page does not exist before the reader asks for it
    </Needs>
  </p>

  <div class="aside">
    <p>
      Not published, by design: prompts, evaluation criteria detail, and content
      frameworks. Competitive material for a live business.
    </p>
  </div>

  <LeadStrand
    slot="after"
    items={[
      {
        slug: 'how-i-built-a-shared-scoring-model',
        note: 'Twelve models on four axes is the same instrument as a scoring model for product investment: make the comparison explicit so the decision survives someone disagreeing with it. The alternative is whoever argues hardest.',
      },
      {
        slug: 'how-i-built-culture-around-paradox',
        note: 'Shipping a two-person product means knowing which quality bars are load-bearing and which are vanity. "Good enough" as a threshold rather than an excuse is what makes a small team fast instead of sloppy.',
      },
    ]}
  />
</DocumentLayout>

<style>
  .answer {
    font-size: 24px !important;
    line-height: 1.52 !important;
    margin-bottom: 40px !important;
  }

  .shot {
    margin: 0 0 34px;
  }
</style>
